      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the code snippet, that makes sense. but i'm still a bit confused why pandas even issues a \"warning\" instead of just doing it or throwing a proper error. why does it seem so indecisive about whether it's a view or a copy?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Pandas is designed with flexibility in mind, so when assigning values to a subset of a dataframe, it has to determine whether the operation should affect the original dataframe (a \"view\") or a new copy (a \"copy\"). When you perform a slice assignment (`df_alpha['column'] = df['column']`), pandas can't determine if the operation is modifying the existing dataframe or creating a duplicate. To be safe, it issues the warning as a best-effort approach, allowing users to verify their intention.\n\nIf the operation is declared as a copy (e.g., using `.copy()` or `.loc`), pandas knows you explicitly want to modify a new object. Also, if the view is verified by cheat codes (`dict.values()[:2]` in the background), the \"view\" is permitted.\n\nThe warning exists because if you want to modify a \"view\" (i.e., no copy), you can do so, but it's up to you (the programmer) to be sure. If you want to work with a copy, use `.copy()`. Many pandas issues are best-effort, leaving users to speculate and figure out whether they really want to work with a view or a copy. This philosophy strives to make pandas",
      "source": "target",
